  "id": "Compound::DB01069 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Promethazine exhibits a strong binding interaction with Hrh4, which is involved in multiple receptor activity pathways, including G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity and neurotransmitter receptor activity. These pathways are closely linked to the function of Chrm2, a receptor that is part of the acetylcholine receptor family. The evidence suggests that Promethazine may modulate Chrm2 through the activation of G-protein coupled receptors, potentially influencing signaling pathways associated with acetylcholine receptor function. The consistent involvement of Hrh4 in multiple receptor-related activities across the three paths indicates a broad mechanism of action, possibly affecting Chrm2 through a common intermediate. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Promethazine and Chrm2, mediated via Hrh4 and its participation in receptor activity pathways. The observed scores suggest a robust and reliable relationship, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the exact nature of the interaction.",
